WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440
A typical GGUF workflow starts with choosing or preparing a model file, then selecting a compatible runtime.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.709
The file may come from a model repository, a trusted release page, or a conversion process.

00:00:13.709 --> 00:00:21.217
After download, the runtime loads the file, reads the metadata, allocates memory, and prepares the tokenizer.

00:00:21.217 --> 00:00:28.313
At that point, the user can test prompts through a command line, web interface, local API, or notebook.

00:00:28.313 --> 00:00:31.482
Several runtime settings shape the experience.

00:00:31.482 --> 00:00:37.544
Context length controls how much prompt and conversation history the model can consider.

00:00:37.544 --> 00:00:46.981
Execution may be CPU-only, GPU-only for supported layers, or hybrid, where some work is offloaded to the GPU and some remains on the CPU.

00:00:46.981 --> 00:00:53.870
Thread settings, batch settings, and memory options can affect speed, responsiveness, and stability.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.934
These are engineering choices, not just performance trivia.

00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:02.343
Testing should include more than whether the model answers once.

00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:10.747
Observe memory use, loading time, prompt processing speed, token generation speed, and output quality for the actual task.

00:01:10.747 --> 00:01:16.121
A model that works well for short chat may be poor for long document analysis.

00:01:16.121 --> 00:01:21.356
A quantization that is fast may lose too much quality for careful reasoning.

00:01:21.356 --> 00:01:26.867
A configuration that fits one machine may fail on another with less VRAM or RAM.

00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:31.138
Documenting the working configuration is part of the workflow.

00:01:31.138 --> 00:01:41.953
Record the model name, source, file hash if available, quantization, runtime version, context length, GPU offload setting, key parameters, and hardware used.

00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:46.500
This makes results reproducible and helps compare variants fairly.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:55.180
Without documentation, local AI experiments quickly become a pile of model files and vague memories about what seemed to work.
